{
  "abstract": "Elliptic translate constructions for Diophantine tuples require pairwise edge functions with even divisor. Their half-divisors can have nonzero two-torsion classes, which complicate both classification and rational specialization. We prove that a real symmetric source with only even-order poles has at most one nonzero edge type at real shifts. The argument uses positivity at real halves and conjugation on nonreal half-fibres, and permits a center with no real half and arbitrary source degree. For rational degree-two spoke sources, two distinct twisted classes therefore force the reciprocal or periodic families; outside the periodic family the anchor must be strong. We also exclude the P4 six-translate pattern, a six-subset of a coset of $C_2\\times C_4$, over every characteristic-zero field for nondegenerate degree-two sources. Earlier work treated its order-four edges only in the untwisted options. We handle the mixed and fully twisted cases with a physical norm-conic invariant and residual tangency equations. Exact resultants leave inhabited quartic or quadratic exceptional loci, all of which fail the edge at the common double of the order-four shifts. Independent Sage and SymPy calculations and reconstructed original-source controls support the algebraic reductions. All pole and degree-drop strata and all two-torsion edge options are retained. These results restrict elliptic source architectures; they neither construct a rational septuple nor exclude isolated rational septuples.",
